  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "Most groove variation lives in the kick between the eighth notes: \"1 e\", \"1 a\", \"e and\". Each of these small combinations has its own feel, and being able to play any of them on demand is what lets you copy a groove from a record or follow the bass player.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Heel up for both strokes of a double, the first from the leg, the second a quick ankle rebound (or a short slide up the pedal) — keep both notes even and the hands completely steady above them.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Every line repeats one combination on 1 and 3 and rests on 2 and 4, so you hear it clearly. Lines 1–4 keep eighth hats so the sixteenths fall between the hand strokes; lines 5–8 switch to sixteenth hats so every kick lines up with a hat stroke. Line 6 is four kicks in a row — keep it soft and even before you go faster.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
